1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,740
Everyone has done this.

2
00:00:02,440 --> 00:00:06,486
Lightning flashes, and we start counting.
One. Two. Three.

3
00:00:07,186 --> 00:00:08,279
Then the thunder.

4
00:00:08,979 --> 00:00:11,747
Light arrives first. Sound arrives later.

5
00:00:11,747 --> 00:00:15,054
The longer the count, the farther away it
struck.

6
00:00:15,754 --> 00:00:19,347
Every three seconds of counting is about
one kilometre.

7
00:00:21,447 --> 00:00:23,893
Long before there were people to count,

8
00:00:23,893 --> 00:00:26,590
one river got the longest count in
history.

9
00:00:27,190 --> 00:00:31,058
And the fish living in it left us a record
of every minute.

10
00:00:32,658 --> 00:00:34,987
It's early morning, probably spring.

11
00:00:35,587 --> 00:00:40,877
Mist hangs over the water. Conifers and
flowering trees crowd both banks.

12
00:00:41,477 --> 00:00:44,644
In the river, a young paddlefish is
feeding.

13
00:00:44,644 --> 00:00:49,467
It swims with its mouth wide open, sieving
the water for tiny food.

14
00:00:50,067 --> 00:00:56,016
Its long, flat snout is covered in sensors
that feel the electric fields of its prey.

15
00:00:56,616 --> 00:01:01,099
Sturgeon cruise along the bottom. It's a
quiet, ordinary morning.

16
00:01:01,899 --> 00:01:04,635
This river is in what is now North Dakota.

17
00:01:05,235 --> 00:01:07,829
Three thousand kilometres to the south,

18
00:01:07,829 --> 00:01:11,953
farther than New York is from Denver, lies
what is now Mexico.

19
00:01:12,553 --> 00:01:16,830
And falling toward the sea there is a rock
about ten kilometres wide.

20
00:01:17,430 --> 00:01:21,666
The crater is still there, buried under
the Yucatán Peninsula.

21
00:01:21,666 --> 00:01:23,648
Scientists call it Chicxulub.

22
00:01:24,248 --> 00:01:27,198
Why this river? Because scientists dug it
up.

23
00:01:27,198 --> 00:01:31,852
And what they found in the fish explains
everything that happened next.

24
00:01:32,752 --> 00:01:33,688
Impact.

25
00:01:34,288 --> 00:01:38,757
For a moment, the flash outshines the Sun.
A crater nearly two

26
00:01:38,757 --> 00:01:43,226
hundred kilometres wide opens in the
seabed. The count begins.

27
00:01:43,826 --> 00:01:48,985
Its light reaches the river in a hundredth
of a second, but the river sees nothing.

28
00:01:49,585 --> 00:01:54,235
The Earth is curved. Three thousand
kilometres of planet are in the way.

29
00:01:54,835 --> 00:01:57,988
To be seen from here, anything at the
crater has to

30
00:01:57,988 --> 00:02:01,264
rise more than eight hundred kilometres
into the sky.

31
00:02:03,064 --> 00:02:08,236
Only the fireball, climbing hundreds of
kilometres high, rises over the edge.

32
00:02:08,836 --> 00:02:12,788
A glow in the south, like a second sunrise
in the wrong place.

33
00:02:13,388 --> 00:02:16,109
No sound. The paddlefish keeps feeding.

34
00:02:17,909 --> 00:02:21,340
The first thing to reach the river doesn't
come through the air.

35
00:02:21,340 --> 00:02:22,734
It comes through the rock.

36
00:02:23,334 --> 00:02:26,174
The impact rings the whole planet like a
bell.

37
00:02:26,774 --> 00:02:29,204
Waves dive down into the hot mantle,

38
00:02:29,204 --> 00:02:33,388
where they travel fastest, and curve back
up toward the river.

39
00:02:33,988 --> 00:02:36,791
The fastest waves push and pull the rock,

40
00:02:36,791 --> 00:02:41,098
like a squeezed spring. Slower ones shake
it from side to side.

41
00:02:41,898 --> 00:02:47,548
At five minutes and forty-seven seconds,
the first wave arrives. The ground jolts.

42
00:02:48,148 --> 00:02:52,602
Birds burst out of the trees. The
paddlefish dives for the bottom.

43
00:02:53,202 --> 00:02:57,809
At ten and a half minutes, a slower wave
shakes everything side to side.

44
00:02:58,409 --> 00:03:02,645
At thirteen minutes, the slowest waves
roll the ground like the sea.

45
00:03:04,445 --> 00:03:07,666
It's the biggest earthquake any river has
ever felt.

46
00:03:08,266 --> 00:03:13,486
And the river does what water in a bathtub
does when it's shaken. It starts to slosh.

47
00:03:14,386 --> 00:03:19,817
Meanwhile, the impact has blasted molten
rock out of the atmosphere, into space.

48
00:03:20,417 --> 00:03:25,180
Billions of droplets fly on great arcs,
hundreds of kilometres high,

49
00:03:25,180 --> 00:03:28,191
higher than the space station orbits
today.

50
00:03:28,791 --> 00:03:31,670
As they fly, they cool into beads of
glass,

51
00:03:31,670 --> 00:03:34,281
each about the size of a grain of sand.

52
00:03:35,081 --> 00:03:36,951
Everyone has seen a shooting star.

53
00:03:37,551 --> 00:03:39,451
Thirteen minutes after impact,

54
00:03:39,451 --> 00:03:42,682
the sky over the river fills with millions
of them.

55
00:03:43,282 --> 00:03:48,357
Every streak is a bead of glass hitting
the air at five kilometres per second.

56
00:03:48,957 --> 00:03:52,465
Some flare and split. Their light flickers
on the water.

57
00:03:53,065 --> 00:03:57,164
For nearly half an hour, the glowing sky
heats the forest below.

58
00:03:58,764 --> 00:04:01,646
Slowed by the air, the beads drift down.

59
00:04:01,646 --> 00:04:04,961
A grain of sand takes most of an hour to
fall.

60
00:04:05,561 --> 00:04:09,192
At about fifty-five minutes, the first
glass hits the river.

61
00:04:09,792 --> 00:04:12,689
Tick. Tick. Like the first drops of rain.

62
00:04:14,789 --> 00:04:17,991
The beads sink, fifteen centimetres every
second.

63
00:04:18,591 --> 00:04:21,882
And the paddlefish, still feeding with its
mouth open,

64
00:04:21,882 --> 00:04:24,931
sieves them out of the water with
everything else.

65
00:04:25,531 --> 00:04:30,655
Inside its gills is a fine comb that
catches food. Now it catches glass.

66
00:04:31,255 --> 00:04:34,384
The beads lodge in its gills, one after
another.

67
00:04:35,384 --> 00:04:39,178
By now the sloshing river has grown into
something terrifying.

68
00:04:39,778 --> 00:04:44,711
The water runs backwards, up the channel,
and rises. At least ten metres.

69
00:04:45,511 --> 00:04:48,987
A wall of water, trees and mud tears up
the valley.

70
00:04:49,587 --> 00:04:53,344
Logs, roots and fish tumble together in
the brown water.

71
00:04:53,944 --> 00:04:56,378
It sweeps the paddlefish and its school
out

72
00:04:56,378 --> 00:04:58,813
of the river and throws them onto the
bank.

73
00:05:00,613 --> 00:05:03,288
The water pulls back. Then it comes again.

74
00:05:03,888 --> 00:05:08,927
Each time it drops, it leaves a layer of
mud, burying the fish where they lie.

75
00:05:09,527 --> 00:05:13,387
And the glass keeps falling, into the mud,
layer after layer.

76
00:05:13,987 --> 00:05:18,768
In less than two hours, more than a metre
of mud and glass fills the channel.

77
00:05:19,768 --> 00:05:21,245
Now, back to the count.

78
00:05:21,845 --> 00:05:27,387
Sound is slow. In air, it covers about a
third of a kilometre every second.

79
00:05:27,987 --> 00:05:32,166
To cross three thousand kilometres, it
needs two and a half hours.

80
00:05:32,766 --> 00:05:34,254
One hour after impact,

81
00:05:34,254 --> 00:05:38,515
the sound has only just reached the coast
of what is now Texas.

82
00:05:39,115 --> 00:05:43,913
Two hours after impact, it's passing over
Nebraska. Still not there.

83
00:05:45,513 --> 00:05:50,015
The river is gone. Dust drifts over fresh
mud. Nothing moves.

84
00:05:50,615 --> 00:05:55,504
Two hours, thirty-two minutes after the
flash, the sound finally arrives.

85
00:05:59,504 --> 00:06:02,743
Nine thousand, one hundred and twenty
seconds.

86
00:06:02,743 --> 00:06:06,756
By the storm rule, that's three thousand
kilometres away.

87
00:06:07,356 --> 00:06:11,087
The longest count in history. And no one
is left to hear it.

88
00:06:11,687 --> 00:06:16,435
In the weeks that follow, soot and dust
darken the sky over the whole planet.

89
00:06:17,035 --> 00:06:20,423
About three in every four kinds of animals
and

90
00:06:20,423 --> 00:06:23,590
plants alive that morning will not
survive.

91
00:06:25,690 --> 00:06:31,056
Sixty-six million years later, the river
is a dry hillside in North Dakota.

92
00:06:31,656 --> 00:06:35,304
Scientists dug into that layer of mud and
found the fish,

93
00:06:35,304 --> 00:06:37,927
still lying where the water dropped them.

94
00:06:38,527 --> 00:06:41,002
Many lie stacked on top of each other,

95
00:06:41,002 --> 00:06:44,715
all pointing the same way, the way the
water was flowing.

96
00:06:45,315 --> 00:06:49,618
Their bodies are complete, fins and all,
buried too fast to rot.

97
00:06:50,218 --> 00:06:54,968
Sturgeon and paddlefish. More than half
have glass beads in their gills.

98
00:06:55,868 --> 00:06:58,733
Fish only breathe in glass if they're
alive.

99
00:06:58,733 --> 00:07:02,966
So they were alive when the glass arrived,
within the first hour.

100
00:07:03,566 --> 00:07:08,586
On the old tree trunks, drops of resin
caught the falling glass and turned

101
00:07:08,586 --> 00:07:13,198
to amber. Its chemistry matches the glass
from the crater in Mexico.

102
00:07:13,798 --> 00:07:17,311
Sealed away from water, those beads are
still glass,

103
00:07:17,311 --> 00:07:19,337
sixty-six million years later.

104
00:07:19,937 --> 00:07:23,439
Some scientists still argue about parts of
this story.

105
00:07:23,439 --> 00:07:26,616
But the glass in the gills is hard to
argue with.

106
00:07:28,216 --> 00:07:30,502
Rocks from space still pass us by.

107
00:07:31,102 --> 00:07:33,918
On April thirteenth, twenty twenty-nine,

108
00:07:33,918 --> 00:07:39,549
an asteroid called Apophis will fly closer
to Earth than many of our satellites.

109
00:07:40,149 --> 00:07:43,527
Next time: what that night will look like
from the ground.

110
00:07:44,527 --> 00:07:47,977
But for one young paddlefish, on one
spring morning,

111
00:07:47,977 --> 00:07:51,427
the count ended at two hours and
thirty-two minutes.

112
00:07:52,027 --> 00:07:56,338
And sixty-six million years later, it's
still telling us what it saw.

113
00:07:56,938 --> 00:08:00,009
A clock written in mud, with glass for its
ticks.

